<p>An effect says what a function may touch. Functions are pure unless they say otherwise.</p>

<h2 id="effects">The effects</h2>
<p>There are nine: <code>alloc</code>, <code>suspend</code>, <code>net</code>, <code>fs</code>, <code>clock</code>, <code>random</code>, <code>env</code>, <code>process</code> and <code>console</code>. Write them after the signature: <code>uses console</code>. An unknown name is BYN6003.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">public void bad() uses teleport { }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6003:</span> `teleport` is not an effect
  --&gt; ef-unknown.byn:1:24

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> public void bad() uses teleport { }
<span class="gut">    │</span>                        <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^ not an effect or an `effect` alias of this module</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: choose one of `alloc`, `suspend`, `net`, `fs`, `clock`, `random`, `env`, `process`,
        `console`, or a declared effect alias</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="declared">Declared and inferred</h2>
<ul>
  <li>A <code>public</code> function must declare every effect it has. Missing one is BYN6002. This includes printing and allocating.</li>
  <li>A private function infers its effects. Nothing to write.</li>
  <li>A public function that calls a <code>uses console</code> function must declare <code>console</code> too.</li>
  <li>An interface method whose implementation allocates must declare <code>uses alloc</code> on the interface (BYN6005).</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-effects-declared"/>
<pre><code class="byn">public void say(string s) {
    print(s)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6002:</span> `say` uses `console` but does not declare it
  --&gt; ef-undeclared.byn:2:5

<span class="gut">  2 │</span>     print(s)
<span class="gut">    │</span>     <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^ uses `console`</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: add `console` to the `uses` clause, or remove the call
<span class="gut">    │</span> 1 │ public void say(string s) uses console {
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │                          +++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre><code class="byn">public i64 grow() {
    let xs: List&lt;i64&gt; = [1]
    return xs.length
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6002:</span> `grow` uses `alloc` but does not declare it
  --&gt; ef-alloc.byn:2:25

<span class="gut">  2 │</span>     let xs: List&lt;i64&gt; = [1]
<span class="gut">    │</span>                         <span class="hl">^^^ uses `alloc`</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: add `alloc` to the `uses` clause, or remove the call
<span class="gut">    │</span> 1 │ public i64 grow() uses alloc {
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │                  +++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="provide">Provide</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>provide name: value { … }</code> supplies a capability for a block. Only <code>allocator</code> handlers work today. See <a href="ref-memory.html#allocators">Memory model</a> for an example.</li>
  <li>Any other handler is BYN2022.</li>
  <li>The old <code>context (…) { }</code> form is BYN6006 and the fix is <code>provide</code>.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code class="byn">provide clock: 1 {
    print("x")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2022:</span> the `clock` handler is not supported in v0.1
  --&gt; ef-handler.byn:1:9

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> provide clock: 1 {
<span class="gut">    │</span>         <span class="hl">^^^^^</span></pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre><code class="byn">context (allocator: Arena()) { print("x") }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6006:</span> `context (…) { }` is now `provide … { }`
  --&gt; ef-context.byn:1:1

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> context (allocator: Arena()) { print("x") }
<span class="gut">    │</span> <span class="hl">^^^^^^^ the input form</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: write `provide allocator: Arena() { }` without the parentheses
<span class="gut">    │</span> 1 │ provide allocator: Arena() { print("x") }
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │ +++++++++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<note>Planned: handlers for <code>clock</code>, <code>random</code> and the other effects, and <code>provide log: … { }</code>.</note>

<h2 id="function-params">Function-typed parameters</h2>
<ul>
  <li>A function that takes a function value does not carry that function’s effects. Calling <code>f(x)</code> inside <code>apply</code> adds nothing to <code>apply</code>.</li>
  <li>The effects are charged to the call that passes the value. A public function that passes a <code>uses console</code> function must declare <code>console</code> (BYN6002).</li>
  <li>Passing a parameter on, as in <code>each(xs, by: f)</code>, adds nothing at that point.</li>
  <li>Anywhere else (a <code>let</code> with a function type), the type’s <code>uses</code> clause is an upper bound. Without one, the bound is pure. A function that exceeds it is BYN6005.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-effects-callback"/>
<pre><code class="byn">public i64 apply(i64 x, by (i64 -&gt; i64) f) =&gt; f(x)

i64 loud(i64 x) {
    print("called")
    return x + 1
}

public i64 run() =&gt; apply(1, by: loud)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6002:</span> `run` uses `console` but does not declare it
  --&gt; ec3.byn:8:21

<span class="gut">  8 │</span> public i64 run() =&gt; <span class="hl">apply(1, by: loud)</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>                     <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ uses `console`</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> add `console` to the `uses` clause, or remove the call
<span class="gut">    │ 8 │</span> public i64 run() <span class="add">uses console </span>=&gt; apply(1, by: loud)
<span class="gut">    │   │</span>                 <span class="add">+++++++++++++</span></pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 loud(i64 x) {
    print("called")
    return x + 1
}

void main() {
    let f: i64 -&gt; i64 = loud
    print($"{f(1)}")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6005:</span> `loud` uses `console`, beyond the bound `pure` declared by `i64 -&gt; i64`
  --&gt; e4.byn:7:25

<span class="gut">  7 │</span>     let f: i64 -&gt; i64 = <span class="hl">loud</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>                         <span class="hl">^^^^ uses `console`</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> add `console` to the function type’s `uses`, or pass a function without it</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Write the bound in the type to allow it: <code>let f: i64 -&gt; i64 uses console = loud</code> compiles.</p>

<h2 id="logging">Logging</h2>
<p><code>log</code> writes a diagnostic line to standard error, so it never mixes with what the program prints. It needs no <code>uses</code> clause, so pure functions may log. The level is a particle argument: <code>as: .debug</code>, <code>.warn</code> or <code>.error</code>. The default is <code>.info</code>. Any other level is BYN2720. <code>print</code> is program output and <code>uses console</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">log("starting")
log("detail", as: .debug)
let n: i64 = 3
log($"count {n}", as: .warn)
print("done")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre>info: starting
debug: detail
warn: count 3</pre>
  <figcaption>Standard error of this program. Standard output holds only <code>done</code>. The check script reads standard output, so this is not an example.</figcaption>
</figure>
<note>Planned: <code>provide log: sink { }</code> to intercept log lines, as the specification describes. Only allocator handlers work today.</note>

<h2 id="capabilities">Package capabilities</h2>
<p>The <code>capabilities</code> list in <code>package.byn</code> is an upper bound on what the whole package may do. A function that uses an effect the list does not name is BYN6004, even when the function declares the effect itself. This package allows only <code>.net</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">Package {
    name:         "local/report",
    version:      "0.1.0",
    edition:      2027,
    kind:         .app,
    capabilities: [.net],
}</code></pre>
<pre><code class="byn">void save(string text) uses fs {
    Io.writeFile("report.txt", text) catch { return }
}

void main() {
    save("x")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6004:</span> `save` uses `fs`, which package `local/report` does not declare in `capabilities`
  --&gt; src/app/main.byn:1:24

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> void save(string text) <span class="hl">uses fs</span> {
<span class="gut">    │</span>                        <span class="hl">^^^^^^^ uses `fs`</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> add `.fs` to `capabilities`, or remove the use</pre>
  <figcaption>The output of <code>bayan run</code> in a project with that manifest, with this program in <code>src/app/main.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The check is transitive: a function that only calls <code>save</code> is charged too. The capabilities in the specification are <code>.fs</code>, <code>.net</code>, <code>.process</code>, <code>.env</code> and <code>.ffi</code>.</p>
